const e=[{id:"sv-1-1041",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"The book’s Control checklist links the end of a project back to the charter. What does it ask the team to confirm?",options:["That the original problem statement was never changed","That the results match the requirements of the business case and improvement goals","That every stakeholder approved the root-cause analysis","That the team size stayed as named in the charter"],answer:1,hint:"The business case is not filed and forgotten.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s Control checklist (p. 578) asks whether results match **the requirements of the business case and improvement goals**."},{title:"Apply",body:"So the business case written in Define becomes the yardstick at closure."}],final:"Results checked against the business case → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the charter is a living document; the problem statement may be updated openly at tollgates.","","Incorrect — root causes are verified with data in Analyze, not approved by stakeholders at closure.","Incorrect — team membership is not the closure test; results against the business case are."],bookRef:"Book p. 578"},{id:"sv-1-1042",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A draft charter scope reads: *“All customer-facing processes, in all regions, for all products.”* What is this an example of?",options:["Scope creep","A project constraint","A disguised solution","A “boil-the-ocean” scope"],answer:3,hint:"Too big on day one vs growing over time.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **boil-the-ocean** scope is far too broad to finish — a common charter mistake."},{title:"Apply",body:"It is too big from the start, unlike scope creep, which grows step by step during the project."}],final:"Too big from the start = boil-the-ocean → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — scope creep is gradual, unapproved growth later; this scope is too big from the start.","Incorrect — a constraint is a fixed limit such as no new headcount, not a scope statement.","Incorrect — a disguised solution hides a fix inside the problem statement.",""],trap:"Scope creep grows during the project; boil-the-ocean is too big at the start.",bookRef:"Book pp. 587–589"},{id:"sv-1-1043",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which question on the book’s **Measure** checklist shows that the project charter is a living document?",options:["Has the Champion signed the control plan?","Has the Problem Statement changed?","Have the financial benefits been realised?","Has the root cause been verified with data?"],answer:1,hint:"A living charter is revisited as data arrives, starting in Measure.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s Measure checklist asks “Has the Problem Statement changed?” (p. 231). Measure data can sharpen or correct the original problem."},{title:"Apply",body:"That question only makes sense if the charter can be updated openly at tollgates."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Control plan and realised benefits are Control checks; root cause is Analyze."}],final:"Measure checklist: “Has the Problem Statement changed?” → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — signing the control plan is a Control-phase hand-over check.","","Incorrect — realised benefits are confirmed only at the end of Control.","Incorrect — root-cause verification is an Analyze-phase check."],bookRef:"Book p. 231"},{id:"sv-1-1044",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the **team** and the **stakeholders** named in a charter is CORRECT?",options:["Stakeholders sign the charter, while team members approve scope changes","Team members and stakeholders are the same list under two names","Team members do project work, while stakeholders are informed or consulted","Stakeholders own the result and remove barriers for the team"],answer:2,hint:"Who works on it vs who must be kept in the loop.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The team section names the Belt, members, process owner and experts — people doing the work."},{title:"Apply",body:"The stakeholder section lists who must be informed or consulted; the Champion owns and signs."}],final:"Team = do the work; stakeholders = informed/consulted → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the Champion signs the charter and approves scope changes.","Incorrect — the charter keeps them as separate elements with different jobs.","","Incorrect — owning the result and removing barriers is the Sponsor / Champion’s role."],bookRef:"Book pp. 587–589"},{id:"sv-1-1045",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements describe common **charter mistakes**?

1. A goal with no baseline or no date.
2. A problem statement that says what, where, when and how big.
3. An out-of-scope list agreed with the Champion.
4. Benefits stated as fact before they are validated.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:3,hint:"Book list of charter mistakes.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 mistake (no baseline/date). 2 good practice. 3 good practice. 4 mistake (benefits as fact)."},{title:"Match the pair",body:"Mistakes: 1 and 4."}],final:"Mistakes: 1 and 4 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statement 2 describes a good problem statement, not a mistake.","Incorrect — statements 2 and 3 describe good practice, not mistakes.","Incorrect — statement 3 is good practice: an agreed out-of-scope list prevents scope creep.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 587–589"},{id:"sv-1-1046",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

Any change to an approved project scope should be a formal decision by the [?], recorded in a new charter version.`,options:["Belt","Champion","customer","team"],answer:1,hint:"Who owns the charter?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Scope changes are a **formal decision by the Champion**, recorded in a new charter version — the defence against scope creep."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"The Belt assesses impact, the team does the work and customers give VOC — none of them approves scope."}],final:"Scope change = Champion decision → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the Belt assesses the impact of a change but does not approve it.","","Incorrect — customers give the VOC; they do not approve project scope changes.","Incorrect — the team does the work; scope decisions belong to the Champion."],bookRef:"Book pp. 587–589"},{id:"sv-1-1047",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which is NOT an effective defence against **scope creep**?",options:["Agreeing clear in-scope and out-of-scope boundaries in the charter","Taking each requested scope change to the Champion as a formal decision","Recording each approved change in a new version of the charter","Quietly absorbing small extra requests to keep stakeholders happy"],answer:3,hint:"Scope creep = slow, unapproved growth. Defences make every change visible and formal.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Scope creep is many small, unapproved additions that dilute effort and delay the finish."},{title:"Apply",body:"Absorbing requests quietly *is* scope creep. The other three options are the book’s defences: boundaries, Champion decision, new charter version."}],final:"Quiet absorption causes scope creep → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — clear boundaries are the first defence against scope creep.","Incorrect — formal Champion decisions are exactly how scope changes should be handled.","Incorrect — versioning the charter keeps scope changes visible and controlled.",""],trap:"Each addition looks small — the problem is that nobody formally decides on it.",bookRef:"Book pp. 587–589"},{id:"sv-1-1048",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A Black Belt presents a charter with a business case, problem and goal statements, scope, milestones and a team list. The sign-off box is blank, and no senior manager has agreed to own the result or remove barriers. Which charter element is effectively missing?",options:["The Sponsor / Champion","The in-scope / out-of-scope list","The risks, assumptions and constraints","The stakeholder list"],answer:0,hint:"Who owns the result, signs the charter and removes barriers?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **Sponsor / Champion** owns the result, signs the charter and removes barriers for the team."},{title:"Apply",body:"A blank sign-off box and nobody owning the result means this element is missing, even though the other sections exist."}],final:"Owner + signature + barrier removal = Sponsor / Champion → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the stem says the scope section is present.","Incorrect — risks and constraints describe threats and limits, not who owns the result.","Incorrect — stakeholders are informed or consulted; they do not sign or own the result."],bookRef:"Book pp. 587–589"},{id:"sv-1-1049",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"During Measure, data show the problem is narrower than first written. The Black Belt quietly edits the problem statement in the shared file and tells nobody. Was this an appropriate action, and why?",options:["Yes, because the charter is a living document","Yes, because Measure data always override the charter","No, because charter changes should be made openly at tollgates, never drift silently","No, because the problem statement can never change after sign-off"],answer:2,hint:"Living document ≠ silent edits.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The charter is a living document, updated **openly** at tollgates (Measure checklist: “Has the Problem Statement changed?”)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Changing it silently removes the Champion’s control, so it was not appropriate."}],final:"Silent edit → not appropriate → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — living means updated openly and agreed, not edited silently.","Incorrect — data inform the change, but the Champion must agree it.","","Incorrect — the book’s Measure checklist asks whether the problem statement has changed; updates are allowed."],bookRef:"Book p. 231"},{id:"sv-1-1050",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"In a project charter, a **risk** needs mitigation, while an **assumption** needs checking.",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Risk, assumption, constraint — three different words.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Risk = uncertain event that could hurt the project → mitigate. Assumption = treated as true without proof → check. Constraint = fixed limit → respect."},{title:"Apply",body:"The statement matches the first two definitions → True."}],final:"Risk → mitigate; assumption → check — True → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the statement is true: risks are uncertain events to mitigate; assumptions are beliefs to verify."],bookRef:"Book pp. 587–589"},{id:"sv-1-1051",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book glossary, what is a **metric**?",options:["The limit a customer places on a product or service characteristic","A measure seen as a key indicator of performance, linked to goals and carefully monitored","The best performance the current process has already demonstrated","The performance level achieved by comparable organisations"],answer:1,hint:"Glossary wording: key indicator, linked to goals, monitored.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book glossary (p. 587) calls a metric a measure considered a **key indicator of performance**, linked to goals or objectives and carefully monitored."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Specification limit, entitlement and benchmark are reference values, not the definition of a metric."}],final:"Metric = key performance indicator linked to goals → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this describes a specification limit, which is the voice of the customer.","","Incorrect — this describes entitlement.","Incorrect — this describes a benchmark."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-1052",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is a project **baseline**?",options:["The performance level the team commits to reach by the goal date","The best performance the current process has ever shown","The current performance of the process, measured before any change","The performance measured during the pilot of the solution"],answer:2,hint:"Baseline = where you start.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **baseline** is current performance measured **before** any change, so that the project can later prove a difference."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Target = where you aim; entitlement = best demonstrated; pilot = after a change."}],final:"Baseline = performance before change → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this is the target in the goal statement.","Incorrect — this is entitlement (best demonstrated performance).","","Incorrect — pilot results are compared with the baseline; they are not the baseline."],bookRef:"Book p. 213"},{id:"sv-1-1053",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is the **primary metric** of a Lean Six Sigma project?",options:["A guard-rail measure that watches for side effects of the change","A money measure used only to report the project savings","An input the team adjusts to test possible solutions","The single measure that best represents the charter problem — the project Y"],answer:3,hint:"Primary = the Y the charter problem is about.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **primary metric** is the one measure that best represents the charter problem — the project **Y**, used in both the problem and goal statements."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Guard-rail = secondary; money = financial; adjustable input = X."}],final:"Primary metric = project Y → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this describes a secondary metric.","Incorrect — this is a financial metric, which supports but does not define the project Y.","Incorrect — this is an X (input), not the output Y.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 116"},{id:"sv-1-1054",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is the main purpose of a **secondary metric** in a project?",options:["To detect harmful side effects of improving the primary metric","To replace the primary metric if the goal is missed","To report the financial value of the project","To give the team a second Y to optimise at the same time"],answer:0,hint:"Secondary metrics are guard-rails.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **secondary (guard-rail) metric** watches for side effects that would make the improvement false — e.g. faster repairs causing more repeat faults."},{title:"Apply",body:"Success means the primary improves **without compromising** the secondary metrics (p. 574)."}],final:"Secondary = guard-rail against side effects → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the primary metric is fixed in the charter; a secondary metric never replaces it to rescue a result.","Incorrect — this is the job of a financial metric and Finance’s benefit evaluation.","Incorrect — the book advises one Y at a time; secondary metrics are protected, not optimised."],bookRef:"Book p. 574"},{id:"sv-1-1055",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is a **lagging** indicator?",options:["A measure that moves before the outcome and gives early warning","A measure that reports what has already happened","A measure that protects against side effects of the change","A measure that expresses performance in money"],answer:1,hint:"Lagging = looks back; leading = looks ahead.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **lagging** indicator reports results after the fact, such as monthly SLA compliance published after the month ends."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Early warning = leading; guard-rail = secondary; money = financial."}],final:"Lagging = reports what already happened → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this describes a leading indicator.","","Incorrect — this describes a secondary (guard-rail) metric.","Incorrect — this describes a financial metric."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1056",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"An operator’s primary metric is **monthly churn** of broadband customers. Which measure is MOST likely to act as a **leading** indicator?",options:["Quarterly revenue lost from cancelled contracts","Annual customer-retention percentage","Weekly number of open complaints older than seven days","Monthly count of cancelled contracts"],answer:2,hint:"A leading indicator moves before the outcome.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **leading** indicator changes **before** the outcome and gives time to act."},{title:"Apply",body:"Old unresolved complaints build up before customers cancel, so they warn of future churn."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Lost revenue, annual retention and cancellations all report churn after it has happened."}],final:"Ageing complaints warn of churn → leading → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — lost revenue is a lagging financial result of churn that already happened.","Incorrect — an annual retention figure lags even further behind than monthly churn.","","Incorrect — this is the churn outcome itself, not an early warning of it."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1057",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A project aims to improve the reliability of enterprise data links. Which is an **outcome** metric for this project?",options:["Time from network alarm to ticket assignment","Service credits paid to customers in OMR","Number of preventive-maintenance visits completed","Percentage of months in which the availability SLA is met"],answer:3,hint:"Outcome = the result the customer or business feels.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Outcome** metrics are results the customer or business feels — SLA compliance, first-contact resolution, churn."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Alarm-to-assignment time and maintenance visits describe the process; credits paid are financial."}],final:"SLA met = outcome metric → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this is a process (in-process) metric describing how the work is done.","Incorrect — this is a financial metric, performance expressed in money.","Incorrect — this counts an activity (a process measure), not the result customers feel.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1058",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which is a **financial** metric for a field-repair project?",options:["Cost of rework per repair job","First-time fix rate","Time to dispatch an engineer","Repeat-fault rate within 30 days"],answer:0,hint:"Financial metric = performance in money.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Financial** metrics express performance in money: credits paid, cost per repair, cost of rework."},{title:"Apply",body:"Only the rework cost is measured in money; the others are outcome or process measures."}],final:"Money measure = financial metric → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — first-time fix is an outcome metric, not measured in money.","Incorrect — dispatch time is a process metric.","Incorrect — repeat faults are an outcome often used as a secondary (guard-rail) metric."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1059",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why should the goal statement use the **same metric** as the problem statement?",options:["So that Finance can classify the benefit as a hard saving","So that progress against the stated problem can be judged directly","So that the team does not need to collect baseline data","So that secondary metrics no longer need to be tracked"],answer:1,hint:"Problem and goal should talk about one Y.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The goal takes the **same metric** as the problem statement and adds a target and a date."},{title:"Apply",body:"If the goal uses a different metric, nobody can tell whether the stated problem was actually solved — a common charter mistake."}],final:"Same metric → result can be judged against the problem → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — benefit classification depends on whether real spend falls, not on metric wording.","","Incorrect — the baseline is still needed; it is the starting point of that same metric.","Incorrect — guard-rail metrics are tracked whatever the goal metric is."],bookRef:"Book pp. 587–589"},{id:"sv-1-1060",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"How is a **secondary metric** applied during a project?",options:["By replacing the primary metric when it is hard to measure","By setting the target value for the primary metric","By checking that gains in the primary metric do not cause harm elsewhere","By converting the primary metric gain into money"],answer:2,hint:"Guard-rail metrics are watched alongside the primary metric.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Secondary metrics are monitored throughout so that a “better” primary metric is not bought with a worse result elsewhere."},{title:"Apply",body:"Example: cut MTTR (primary) while watching the repeat-incident rate (secondary)."}],final:"Secondary metric = side-effect check → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a hard-to-measure Y needs a better operational definition, not a swap to a guard-rail.","Incorrect — targets come from the baseline, entitlement and benchmarks.","","Incorrect — this is benefit valuation, done with Finance."],bookRef:"Book p. 574"},{id:"sv-1-1061",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does the book advise working on **one output (Y) at a time**?",options:["Statistical tools can analyse only one variable at a time","Finance accepts savings from only one metric per project","The charter has room for only one metric","Trying to improve several outputs at once quickly becomes overwhelming"],answer:3,hint:"Focus beats spread.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 116) says to solve **one output at a time**; chasing several Ys at once becomes overwhelming."},{title:"Apply",body:"Other important measures become secondary (guard-rail) metrics, not extra primary metrics."}],final:"One Y at a time — several at once is overwhelming → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — many tools (regression, ANOVA) handle several variables; the advice is about focus.","Incorrect — there is no such Finance rule; the advice is about project focus.","Incorrect — the charter lists a primary and secondary metrics.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 116"},{id:"sv-1-1062",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why should a project baseline be reported from **long-term** data?",options:["Long-term data includes the normal variation of busy and quiet periods","Long-term data always shows better performance than short-term data","Short-term data cannot be plotted on a control chart","Long-term data removes the need for an operational definition"],answer:0,hint:"A baseline must represent normal operation.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 213) says always use **long-term data** to report the baseline."},{title:"Apply",body:"Long periods capture month-ends, shifts and peaks; one good week overstates performance."}],final:"Long-term data captures normal variation → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — long-term data usually shows more variation, often worse performance, which is more honest.","Incorrect — short-term data can be charted; it just misses normal sources of variation.","Incorrect — every baseline needs an operational definition, whatever the period."],bookRef:"Book p. 213"},{id:"sv-1-1063",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Why should each project metric state its **direction**, for example “lower is better”?",options:["So that the metric can be converted into money","So that everyone reads a change in the metric as better or worse in the same way","So that the baseline period can be shortened","So that the metric becomes a leading indicator"],answer:1,hint:"Breaches: lower is better. First-time fix: higher is better.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Stating the **direction** removes confusion about whether a rise is good news or bad news."},{title:"Apply",body:"A rising breach rate is bad; a rising first-time-fix rate is good — the reader must know which."}],final:"Direction makes improvement unambiguous → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — direction says what counts as improvement; valuation in money is a separate step.","","Incorrect — the baseline period depends on normal variation, not on direction.","Incorrect — whether a metric leads or lags depends on timing, not on its direction."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1064",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In the book’s DMAIC phase reports, which two metric headings appear in **every** report?",options:["Hard Savings and Soft Savings","Leading Indicator and Lagging Indicator","Primary Metric and Secondary Metric(s)","Entitlement and Benchmark"],answer:2,hint:"The book treats two metrics as constant companions.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Every phase report in the book lists **“Primary Metric”** and **“Secondary Metric(s)”** (pp. 229, 416, 488)."},{title:"Apply",body:"The pair travels through every tollgate, so the guard-rail is never forgotten."}],final:"Phase reports: Primary + Secondary metric(s) → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — savings categories are a Finance classification, not the standing metric headings.","Incorrect — leading/lagging is a way to classify metrics, not the report headings.","","Incorrect — these are reference points for setting a target."],bookRef:"Book pp. 229, 416, 488"},{id:"sv-1-1065",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"While building the **X-Y matrix**, a team finds an input that strongly affects a customer result that the project is not tracking. According to the book, what can this lead to?",options:["Dropping the primary metric from the charter","Closing the project because the scope is wrong","Turning the input into the project’s financial metric","Adding a secondary metric or refining the primary metric"],answer:3,hint:"The X-Y matrix links inputs to outputs — and can reveal missing outputs.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 120) notes that building the X-Y matrix may show the need to **add a secondary metric or refine the primary metric**."},{title:"Apply",body:"An untracked customer result affected by a key X is exactly such a missing secondary metric."}],final:"X-Y matrix can add/refine metrics → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the project Y stays; the matrix may add a guard-rail or sharpen the Y.","Incorrect — a new insight leads to a metric update, not to closing the project.","Incorrect — an input (X) is not a financial measure of the project result.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 120"},{id:"sv-1-1066",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Percentage improvement in a project metric is always expressed relative to which value?",options:["The baseline","The target","The new (after) value","The entitlement"],answer:0,hint:"Improvement is measured from the starting point.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Percentage improvement always divides by the **baseline** (the starting value)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Dividing by the new value is the common exam trap."}],final:"Divide by the baseline → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the target is the aim; improvement is measured from where the process started.","Incorrect — dividing by the new value is the classic trap and overstates the improvement.","Incorrect — entitlement is a reference for ambition, not the denominator of improvement."],trap:"Dividing by the after-value overstates the gain.",bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1067",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which BEST describes the **target** for a project’s primary metric?",options:["The best performance the current process has demonstrated","The level the team commits to reach by a stated date","The level of performance before any change","The level that comparable organisations achieve"],answer:1,hint:"Baseline = start; target = destination.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **target** is the committed level of the primary metric, with a date, written into the goal statement."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Entitlement and benchmark inform the target; the baseline is the start."}],final:"Target = committed level + date → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this is entitlement, a reference point for the target.","","Incorrect — this is the baseline.","Incorrect — this is a benchmark, another reference point."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1068",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about a project **baseline** is CORRECT?",options:["The baseline should be taken from the best recent week of data","The baseline is set after the pilot, from the improved results","Without a baseline, the team cannot prove that the project made a difference","A baseline is only needed when the target is not ambitious"],answer:2,hint:"What is the result compared with at the end?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The baseline is the before-picture; every claim of improvement is a comparison with it."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Best week = biased; after the pilot = not a baseline; the baseline is never optional."}],final:"No baseline → no proof → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — one good week overstates performance; use long-term data.","Incorrect — pilot results are compared with the baseline, measured before change.","","Incorrect — every project needs a baseline, whatever the target."],bookRef:"Book p. 213"},{id:"sv-1-1069",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about **primary and secondary metrics** is CORRECT?",options:["Secondary metrics must improve by the same amount as the primary metric","A project may have several primary metrics but only one secondary metric","Secondary metrics are chosen at the Control tollgate","A project succeeds when the primary metric improves without compromising the secondary metrics"],answer:3,hint:"Book Control checklist wording.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s Control checklist asks whether the primary metric improved **without compromising the secondary metrics** (p. 574)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Secondary = protected, not improved; one primary; chosen early."}],final:"Primary up, secondary not compromised = success → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — secondary metrics must not get worse; they do not need to improve.","Incorrect — it is the other way round: one primary Y, one or more secondary metrics.","Incorrect — they are set early (charter/Define) and reported in every phase.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 574"},{id:"sv-1-1070",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about **leading and lagging** indicators is CORRECT?",options:["Process metrics usually lead, while outcome metrics usually lag","Outcome metrics usually give the earliest warning of a problem","A lagging indicator moves before the outcome it predicts","Leading indicators are used only after the project closes"],answer:0,hint:"Process changes first; the outcome follows.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Process metrics (how work is done) usually **lead**; outcome metrics (results felt) usually **lag**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Good projects pair them: an outcome primary metric, plus process metrics for steering between reports."}],final:"Process leads, outcome lags → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — outcomes usually arrive later; process metrics give the early warning.","Incorrect — this is the definition of a leading indicator.","Incorrect — leading indicators are most useful during the project, for steering."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1071",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about **entitlement** is CORRECT?",options:["It is the average performance of comparable organisations","It is a performance level that the current process has already demonstrated","It is the limit set by the customer for the characteristic","It is the process performance measured before any change"],answer:1,hint:"Entitlement = best demonstrated performance.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book glossary (p. 585) defines entitlement as the **best demonstrated performance** of the current process."},{title:"Apply",body:"If one region already achieves it with the same process, that level is clearly possible."}],final:"Entitlement = best already demonstrated → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this describes a benchmark.","","Incorrect — this describes a specification limit.","Incorrect — this describes the baseline."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-1072",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about comparing **raw counts** between periods is CORRECT?",options:["A rising count always shows that performance has become worse","Rates should be used only when volume stays constant","Counts should be normalised by volume before periods are compared, when volume has changed","Normalising hides the real size of the problem"],answer:2,hint:"Fair units: per 1,000 subscribers, per order, per call.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Raw counts mislead when volume changes; normalise (e.g. complaints per 1,000 subscribers) before comparing."},{title:"Apply",body:"A count can go up while the rate improves."}],final:"Normalise before comparing → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — counts can rise only because volume grew while the rate improved.","Incorrect — rates matter most when volume changes.","","Incorrect — normalising shows the true rate; the count can still be reported beside it."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1073",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about an **operational definition** for a project metric is CORRECT?",options:["It is written after the baseline has been reported","It is needed only for discrete (attribute) data","It sets the target value for the metric","It states exactly what counts and when measurement starts and stops"],answer:3,hint:"Does the restore clock start at the alarm, the ticket or the call?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"An operational definition says exactly **what counts** and where the clock **starts and stops**, so everyone measures the same thing."},{title:"Apply",body:"It is the first of three baseline decisions (definition, period, data source)."}],final:"Operational definition = exact what / start / stop → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the definition comes first; otherwise the baseline data are inconsistent.","Incorrect — continuous measures such as restore time need clear start and stop points too.","Incorrect — the target is set in the goal statement, not in the definition.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 213"},{id:"sv-1-1074",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about **secondary metrics** are CORRECT?

1. A secondary metric watches for side effects of the improvement.
2. A secondary metric must improve by at least as much as the primary metric.
3. Secondary metrics are chosen only after the project closes.
4. Phase reports list secondary metrics alongside the primary metric.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:3,hint:"Guard-rail, protected not optimised, reported every phase.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 true (guard-rail). 2 false (must not worsen; no need to improve). 3 false (chosen early). 4 true (pp. 229, 416, 488)."},{title:"Match the pair",body:"True: 1 and 4."}],final:"True: 1 and 4 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statement 2 is false: a secondary metric must not get worse; it need not improve.","Incorrect — statements 2 and 3 are false: secondary metrics are protected, and chosen early.","Incorrect — statement 3 is false: secondary metrics are set early and reported in every phase.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 229, 574"},{id:"sv-1-1075",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about **baseline data** are CORRECT?

1. One unusually good week is a sound basis for the baseline.
2. The baseline period should include busy and quiet periods.
3. The baseline needs a clearly stated data source and population.
4. The baseline is measured after the solution has been piloted.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:1,hint:"Long-term, representative, clearly sourced, before change.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 false (one week is not a baseline). 2 true (normal variation). 3 true (source, population, exclusions). 4 false (before change)."},{title:"Match the pair",body:"True: 2 and 3."}],final:"True: 2 and 3 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statement 1 is false: one good week overstates performance.","","Incorrect — statement 4 is false: the baseline is measured before any change.","Incorrect — statements 1 and 4 are both false."],bookRef:"Book p. 213"},{id:"sv-1-1076",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:3,stem:`Which statements about **units** in project metrics are CORRECT?

1. Baseline, target and benefit should use the same time units.
2. Each metric should state whether lower or higher is better.
3. A fall in error rate from 20% to 15% is best described as a 5% improvement.
4. Percentage improvement is found by dividing by the new value.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:0,hint:"Same time units, stated direction, points ≠ percent, divide by baseline.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 true. 2 true. 3 false — that is 5 **percentage points**, a larger relative improvement. 4 false — divide by the **baseline**."},{title:"Match the pair",body:"True: 1 and 2."}],final:"True: 1 and 2 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — statement 3 is false: it is a 5 percentage-point fall, not a 5% improvement.","Incorrect — statements 3 and 4 are both false (points vs percent; divide by baseline).","Incorrect — statement 4 is false: always divide by the baseline."],trap:"Percentage points and percent are not the same thing.",bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1077",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:3,stem:`Which statements about types of project metric are CORRECT?

1. Monthly SLA compliance is a leading indicator.
2. Lagging indicators are mainly used for daily steering.
3. Process metrics often give early warning of outcome problems.
4. The primary metric is usually an outcome metric.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:2,hint:"Outcome usually lags; process usually leads.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 false (it lags). 2 false (lagging = too late to steer daily). 3 true. 4 true."},{title:"Match the pair",body:"True: 3 and 4."}],final:"True: 3 and 4 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statements 1 and 2 are both false: monthly SLA compliance lags, and lagging data is too late for daily steering.","Incorrect — statement 2 is false: lagging indicators arrive too late for daily steering.","","Incorrect — statement 1 is false: monthly SLA compliance is published after the month, so it lags."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1078",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

[?] is the best demonstrated performance of the current process.`,options:["Baseline","Benchmark","Entitlement","Target"],answer:2,hint:"Book glossary p. 585.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary (p. 585) defines **entitlement** as the best demonstrated performance of the current process."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Baseline = typical now; benchmark = others; target = commitment."}],final:"Best demonstrated = entitlement → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — baseline is current (typical) performance, not the best demonstrated.","Incorrect — a benchmark is what comparable organisations achieve.","","Incorrect — the target is the committed future level in the goal statement."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-1079",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

A [?] indicator moves before the outcome and gives early warning.`,options:["financial","lagging","leading","secondary"],answer:2,hint:"Timing lens: leading vs lagging.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **leading** indicator moves first — e.g. P1 tickets still unassigned after 15 minutes predict SLA breaches later that day."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Lagging is the opposite; financial and secondary describe other properties."}],final:"Moves first = leading → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a financial metric is defined by its unit (money), not by its timing.","Incorrect — a lagging indicator reports results after they happen.","","Incorrect — a secondary metric is a guard-rail; it may lead or lag."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1080",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:2,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

The book’s Control checklist asks whether the project improved the primary metric without compromising the [?] metrics.`,options:["baseline","financial","process","secondary"],answer:3,hint:"Primary vs its guard-rails.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Control checklist (p. 574): improve the primary metric **without compromising the secondary metrics**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Secondary metrics are the guard-rails that must not get worse."}],final:"Without compromising the secondary metrics → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the baseline is a starting value, not a set of metrics to protect.","Incorrect — the checklist wording refers to the guard-rail metrics, not money measures.","Incorrect — process metrics help steering; the checklist protects the secondary metrics.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 574"},{id:"sv-1-1081",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

The book advises always using [?] data to report the baseline.`,options:["long-term","pilot","short-term","subgroup"],answer:0,hint:"Baseline must capture normal variation.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 213) says always use **long-term data** to report the baseline."},{title:"Apply",body:"Long-term data includes peaks, month-ends and shift effects."}],final:"Baseline = long-term data → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — pilot data describes the process after a change.","Incorrect — short-term data misses normal variation and overstates performance.","Incorrect — subgroups are a sampling structure, not a statement about the baseline period."],bookRef:"Book p. 213"},{id:"sv-1-1082",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which is NOT one of the decisions needed to build a credible baseline?",options:["Choosing the improvement target","Writing an operational definition of the metric","Choosing a period that includes normal variation","Agreeing the data source, population and exclusions"],answer:0,hint:"Three baseline decisions: definition, period, source.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A credible baseline needs an **operational definition**, a representative **period** and an agreed **data source**."},{title:"Apply",body:"The target is set **after** the baseline is known; it is not part of building the baseline."}],final:"Target comes after the baseline → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the operational definition is the first baseline decision.","Incorrect — the period is the second baseline decision.","Incorrect — the data source is the third baseline decision."],bookRef:"Book p. 213"},{id:"sv-1-1083",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"Which is NOT a **process** metric for an enterprise fault-repair project?",options:["Time from network alarm to ticket assignment","Monthly SLA compliance reported to customers","Percentage of tickets with a complete fault description","Time from assignment to engineer arrival on site"],answer:1,hint:"Process = how work is done; outcome = result felt.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Process metrics describe steps inside the work; outcome metrics describe the result customers feel."},{title:"Apply",body:"SLA compliance is the **outcome** (and lags); the other three are in-process measures."}],final:"SLA compliance = outcome, not process → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this measures how the work is done, so it is a process metric.","","Incorrect — ticket quality inside the process is a process metric.","Incorrect — this is an in-process step time, a process metric."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1084",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"Which is NOT a reference point the book gives for setting a project target?",options:["The baseline","The entitlement","The control limits of the current process","A benchmark"],answer:2,hint:"Three reference points: now, best demonstrated, others.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Targets are set from the **baseline**, the **entitlement** and **benchmarks**; breakthrough (≈ 70%) reminds the team to be ambitious."},{title:"Apply",body:"Control limits describe the voice of the process (its natural variation); they are not a goal."}],final:"Control limits are not a target reference → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the baseline (where you are now) is a reference point for the target.","Incorrect — entitlement (best demonstrated) is a reference point for the target.","","Incorrect — benchmarks (what comparable organisations achieve) are a reference point."],bookRef:"Book pp. 584–585"},{id:"sv-1-1085",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"A good project metric has all of these characteristics EXCEPT:",options:["It is linked to the project’s goals","It has a clear operational definition","It traces back to a CTQ","It is chosen because it is the easiest data to collect"],answer:3,hint:"A metric is chosen for relevance, not convenience.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Good metrics are linked to goals, clearly defined and traced to a CTQ."},{title:"Apply",body:"Ease of collection is a practical bonus, never the reason to choose a metric that may not represent the problem."}],final:"Convenience is not a selection criterion → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the glossary requires a metric to be linked to goals or objectives.","Incorrect — a clear definition is needed for a consistent baseline.","Incorrect — every project metric should trace to a CTQ (need → CTQ → metric).",""],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-1086",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A packing-line project aims to cut the **cycle time per carton**. Operators warn that rushing could increase damage to goods in transit. Which measure should the Black Belt add as a **secondary** metric?",options:["Percentage of shipments arriving damaged","Cycle time per carton","Number of operators per shift","Cartons packed per hour"],answer:0,hint:"Which result could get worse because of the improvement?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A secondary metric watches the side effect the improvement might cause."},{title:"Apply",body:"Faster packing risks more damage, so % damaged shipments is the guard-rail."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Cycle time is the Y; operators is an input; throughput simply mirrors the Y."}],final:"Guard-rail = damaged shipments % → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — this is the primary metric itself.","Incorrect — this is an input (X) or resource level, not a guard-rail result.","Incorrect — this moves with cycle time, so it cannot warn of the feared side effect."],bookRef:"Book p. 574"},{id:"sv-1-1087",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"In the business case, a Black Belt quotes an industry survey showing that comparable operators restore **92%** of P1 incidents within SLA. Her own process restores 81%. What does the 92% figure represent?",options:["The entitlement","A benchmark","The baseline","The secondary metric"],answer:1,hint:"Inside the process vs outside the organisation.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **benchmark** is what comparable organisations achieve."},{title:"Apply",body:"The 92% comes from other operators, so it is a benchmark, not her entitlement or baseline."}],final:"Other operators’ level = benchmark → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — entitlement is the best performance already demonstrated by her own current process.","","Incorrect — her baseline is her own current performance, 81%.","Incorrect — a secondary metric is a guard-rail measure, not an external comparison value."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-1088",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A project’s primary metric is **monthly SLA compliance**, which is published five days after each month ends. The Champion wants to steer the work week by week. Which additional metric is BEST?",options:["Quarterly service-credit cost","Annual customer-satisfaction survey score","Daily count of P1 tickets still unassigned after 15 minutes","Monthly SLA compliance for the previous year"],answer:2,hint:"Steering needs a leading process metric.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Lagging outcomes arrive too late to steer; a **leading** process metric moves first."},{title:"Apply",body:"Unassigned P1 tickets today predict breaches later, so the team can react within the week."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Credits, annual surveys and last year’s compliance all lag."}],final:"Unassigned-after-15-min = leading steering metric → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this is a lagging financial result, even slower than the monthly report.","Incorrect — an annual survey lags far too much for weekly steering.","","Incorrect — historic data of the same lagging metric gives no early warning."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1089",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A charter problem says repairs take too long: mean time to restore is well above the 4-hour target. The draft names **training hours delivered to engineers** as the primary metric. What is the main weakness?",options:["It is a lagging indicator","It cannot be normalised by volume","It is a financial metric","It measures an activity, not the problem the project must move"],answer:3,hint:"Primary metric = the charter problem’s Y.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The primary metric must represent the charter problem — here, restore time."},{title:"Apply",body:"Training hours are an activity (and a possible solution); they can rise while restore time stays the same."}],final:"Activity ≠ project Y → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the issue is not timing; the metric does not represent restore time at all.","Incorrect — normalisation is not the issue; the metric does not represent the problem.","Incorrect — training hours are not measured in money.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 116"},{id:"sv-1-1090",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"The October SLA compliance report for enterprise customers is published on 5 November. How is this metric BEST classified?",options:["A lagging outcome indicator","A leading process indicator","A leading outcome indicator","A lagging process indicator"],answer:0,hint:"Two lenses: what it describes (outcome/process) and when it arrives (leading/lagging).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"SLA compliance is a result the customer feels → **outcome**."},{title:"Apply",body:"It is published after the month ends → **lagging**."}],final:"SLA compliance = lagging outcome → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — it neither moves early nor describes how the work is done.","Incorrect — it is an outcome, but it arrives after the month, so it lags.","Incorrect — it lags, but SLA compliance is a result customers feel, an outcome."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1091",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"Enterprise customers say: *“My bill must be right first time.”* Which is the BEST primary metric for the project?",options:["Number of billing staff trained this quarter","Percentage of bills issued with one or more errors","Average time to print and send a bill","Total billing revenue per month"],answer:1,hint:"Need → CTQ → primary metric.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Trace the need to a CTQ (*bill free of errors*) and measure it directly."},{title:"Apply",body:"% of bills with at least one error measures exactly that CTQ."}],final:"Accuracy need → % bills with errors → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this counts an activity, not the customer’s requirement.","","Incorrect — this is about speed; the customer need is accuracy.","Incorrect — revenue is a financial result and does not show bill accuracy."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1092",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"The baseline breach rate is **20%**. A draft goal says: *“Reduce breaches by 10% by June.”* The Champion says the goal is not clear. What is the problem?",options:["A goal cannot be expressed as a percentage","It uses the baseline instead of the entitlement","It does not say whether it means 10 percentage points or a 10% relative reduction","It should name the solution that will reduce breaches"],answer:2,hint:"Points vs percent.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"When a metric is itself a percentage, “by 10%” is ambiguous."},{title:"Apply",body:"It could mean 10 percentage points or a 10% relative fall — two very different targets. Write the target value explicitly."}],final:"Ambiguous points vs percent → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — percentage targets are normal if their meaning is precise.","Incorrect — referring to the baseline is correct; the problem is the unit wording.","","Incorrect — a goal states what, not how."],trap:"Say “percentage points” or give the target value.",bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1093",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"Straight after charter approval, the Champion asks for a target for repeat calls before any data has been collected. The Black Belt instead first agrees an operational definition and measures a baseline, then proposes the target. Was this an appropriate action, and why?",options:["Yes, because a target can only be judged against a measured baseline","Yes, because targets are set by the Black Belt, not by the Champion","No, because the Champion’s target must be accepted as written","No, because the baseline is measured in the Control phase"],answer:0,hint:"What is established FIRST?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The baseline (with an operational definition) comes first; then the target."},{title:"Apply",body:"The Belt followed that order, so the action was appropriate."}],final:"Baseline before target = appropriate → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the Champion approves the goal; the reason is sequence, not authority.","Incorrect — a target with no baseline cannot be judged for ambition or realism.","Incorrect — the baseline is measured before change, in Measure."],bookRef:"Book p. 213"},{id:"sv-1-1094",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A project cut invoice processing time by 30%, but invoice accuracy (the secondary metric) fell from 98% to 93%. The Black Belt reported the project as a success at the Control tollgate. Was this an appropriate action, and why?",options:["Yes, because the primary metric beat its target","Yes, because secondary metrics are for information only","No, because success requires the primary gain without compromising the secondary metric","No, because accuracy should have been the primary metric"],answer:2,hint:"Book Control checklist: without compromising…",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The primary metric must improve **without compromising** the secondary metrics (p. 574)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Accuracy fell sharply, so the “improvement” is not a success yet."}],final:"Guard-rail broken → not appropriate → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — beating the primary target is not enough if a guard-rail metric got worse.","Incorrect — secondary metrics are part of the definition of success (p. 574).","","Incorrect — the choice of primary metric was fine; the guard-rail was broken."],bookRef:"Book p. 574"},{id:"sv-1-1095",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"The Champion proposes a goal of raising on-time repair from **70% to 72%**. Data show that one region already achieves **90%** with the same process and tools. What is the BEST action for the Black Belt?",options:["Accept 72% because small goals are easier to meet","Set the target at 100% to signal breakthrough","Delay the target until other operators’ benchmarks arrive","Use the 90% entitlement to propose a more ambitious target"],answer:3,hint:"Entitlement shows what is clearly possible.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Entitlement = best demonstrated performance of the current process (p. 585)."},{title:"Apply",body:"A region at 90% proves much more than 72% is achievable, so the target should be raised."}],final:"Use entitlement to set an ambitious target → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a target far below demonstrated performance wastes the project’s potential.","Incorrect — 100% is not demonstrated; targets should be ambitious but grounded in evidence.","Incorrect — entitlement data is already in hand; there is no need to wait.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 584–585"},{id:"sv-1-1096",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"For an incident-restoration project, the Black Belt reports the baseline both as the average restore time (3.5 h) and as the % of incidents restored after the 4-hour limit. Was this an appropriate action, and why?",options:["Yes, because an average within the limit can hide many incidents beyond it","Yes, because two primary metrics are better than one","No, because time metrics should be reported only as averages","No, because percentages are not allowed in a baseline"],answer:0,hint:"Time CTQ: average + % meeting the limit.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"For time CTQs, also report the **% meeting the limit**; a mean of 3.5 h can hide many incidents over 4 h."},{title:"Apply",body:"Reporting both was therefore appropriate."}],final:"Mean + % beyond limit = appropriate → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the project still has one primary metric; the % view describes the same CTQ.","Incorrect — averages alone can hide the tail that breaches the limit.","Incorrect — percentages are normal baseline measures."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1097",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`All four regions use the same repair process and tools. A Black Belt tabulates last year’s results:

\`\`\`
Tabulated Statistics: Region; On-time repair (%)

Region   Repairs   On-time %
North      1,240        71.2
South        980        88.6
East       1,105        74.9
West         860        69.4
All        4,185        75.9
\`\`\`

Which value BEST represents the **entitlement** for on-time repair?`,options:["88.6%","75.9%","69.4%","100%"],answer:0,hint:"Entitlement = best demonstrated by the current process.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"South already achieves 88.6% with the same process and tools."},{title:"Apply",body:"That is the best demonstrated performance → entitlement. The “All” row (75.9%) is the baseline."}],final:"Best demonstrated region = 88.6% → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — 75.9% is the overall current performance, the baseline.","Incorrect — 69.4% is the worst region, not the best demonstrated.","Incorrect — 100% has never been demonstrated by this process."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-1098",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`The goal is to cut average handle time (AHT) by **60 seconds**. After a script change, the team compares 4,000 calls before and after (α = 0.05):

\`\`\`
Two-Sample T-Test and CI: AHT_Before, AHT_After

Sample        N   Mean  StDev  SE Mean
AHT_Before 4000  312.4   58.1     0.92
AHT_After  4000  309.6   57.4     0.91

Difference = μ (AHT_Before) − μ (AHT_After)
Estimate for difference:  2.80
95% CI for difference:  (0.27, 5.33)
T-Value = 2.17  P-Value = 0.030  DF = 7997
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is BEST?`,options:["The reduction is not statistically significant, so nothing has changed","The reduction is statistically significant but far too small to matter against the goal","The goal has been met because the p-value is below 0.05","The test is invalid because the samples are too large"],answer:1,hint:"Statistical vs practical significance (book p. 285).",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"p = 0.030 < 0.05 → reject H₀; the estimated reduction is only 2.80 s (CI 0.27 to 5.33 s)."},{title:"Compare with the goal",body:"The goal is 60 s; even the upper CI limit is far below it."},{title:"Conclude",body:"Statistically significant, but not practically significant."}],final:"Significant but not practically significant → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — p = 0.030 < 0.05, so the small difference is statistically significant.","","Incorrect — significance shows a real difference, not that the 60-second goal was reached.","Incorrect — large samples are valid; they simply detect very small differences."],bookRef:"Book p. 285"},{id:"sv-1-1099",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`The primary metric is MTTR (target **≤ 5.0 h**); the repeat-fault rate is the secondary metric. Four pilots ran for 30 days each:

\`\`\`
Pilot results (30 days each)

Option     MTTR (h)   Repeat-fault rate (%)
Baseline     6.4             8.1
Pilot A      4.1            13.6
Pilot B      4.6             7.9
Pilot C      6.2             5.0
Pilot D      3.9            11.2
\`\`\`

Which pilot BEST meets the charter’s definition of success?`,options:["Pilot A","Pilot B","Pilot C","Pilot D"],answer:1,hint:"Primary meets target AND secondary not worse.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"A and D meet the MTTR target but raise repeat faults; C fails the MTTR target."},{title:"Apply",body:"Only Pilot B meets 5.0 h (4.6 h) with repeat faults no worse than baseline (7.9% vs 8.1%)."}],final:"Pilot B: primary met, guard-rail held → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — MTTR meets the target, but repeat faults rose sharply from 8.1% to 13.6%.","","Incorrect — repeat faults improved, but MTTR (6.2 h) misses the 5.0 h target.","Incorrect — the best MTTR, but repeat faults rose from 8.1% to 11.2%."],bookRef:"Book p. 574"},{id:"sv-1-1100",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt prepares the baseline for SIM activation time:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: Activation time (h)

Period                 N   Mean  StDev  Median  Maximum
Week 32 (quiet)      142    2.9    1.1     2.7      7.8
Jan–Jun 2026       3,860    4.6    2.4     4.1     19.5
Pilot (new process)  210    2.4    0.9     2.3      6.1
\`\`\`

Which mean should be reported as the **baseline** in the problem statement?`,options:["2.9 h, from the quiet week 32","2.4 h, from the pilot","4.6 h, from January–June 2026","19.5 h, the worst case observed"],answer:2,hint:"Baseline = long-term, before change.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Only Jan–Jun 2026 is long-term (N = 3,860) and before the change."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Week 32 is short-term; the pilot is after change; the maximum is one extreme value."}],final:"Long-term, pre-change mean = 4.6 h → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — one quiet week is short-term data and overstates performance.","Incorrect — pilot data reflects the new process, after a change.","","Incorrect — a maximum is a single extreme value, not the typical baseline performance."],bookRef:"Book p. 213"},{id:"sv-1-1101",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt compares billing complaints by region:

\`\`\`
Tabulated Statistics: Region; Billing complaints (Q3 2026)

Region    Complaints  Subscribers  Complaints per 1,000
Capital        2,150      410,000                   5.2
Coastal          980       92,000                  10.7
Interior         640       88,000                   7.3
South            720      131,000                   5.5
\`\`\`

The project should start with the region that has the worst complaint **rate**. Which region is it?`,options:["Capital","Coastal","Interior","South"],answer:1,hint:"Fair units: compare rates, not raw counts.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"The normalised column shows Coastal at 10.7 complaints per 1,000 — the highest."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Capital leads on raw count only because it is the biggest region."}],final:"Highest rate per 1,000 = Coastal → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Capital has the most complaints, but the lowest rate (5.2 per 1,000).","","Incorrect — Interior’s rate is 7.3 per 1,000, below Coastal’s 10.7.","Incorrect — South’s rate is 5.5 per 1,000, one of the lowest."],trap:"The biggest count is not the worst rate.",bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1102",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"**Entitlement** is the performance level achieved by comparable organisations.",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Inside vs outside: entitlement vs benchmark.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Entitlement = best demonstrated performance of the **current process** (p. 585)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Performance of comparable organisations is a **benchmark**, so the statement is false."}],final:"That is a benchmark — False → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this definition belongs to a benchmark; entitlement is the best performance the current process itself has demonstrated.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-1103",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"An improvement can be statistically significant and still have little practical value to the business.",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Statistical vs practical significance.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"With enough data, very small differences become statistically significant."},{title:"Apply",body:"The book (p. 285) says the improvement must also have real business value."}],final:"Significant ≠ valuable — True → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the statement is true: the book warns that statistical significance is not the same as practical significance."],bookRef:"Book p. 285"},{id:"sv-1-1104",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:1,stem:`Put these in the order used to link a project metric to the customer:

- **P** Primary metric
- **Q** Customer need
- **R** Target
- **S** CTQ`,options:["S → Q → P → R","Q → P → S → R","P → R → Q → S","Q → S → P → R"],answer:3,hint:"Need → CTQ → metric → target.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Every project metric traces back to a CTQ: **need → CTQ → primary metric → target**."},{title:"Apply",body:"Q (need) → S (CTQ) → P (metric) → R (target)."}],final:"Need → CTQ → metric → target → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the CTQ is derived from the customer need, so the need comes first.","Incorrect — the metric measures the CTQ, so the CTQ comes before the metric.","Incorrect — starting from the metric loses the link to what the customer values.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1105",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these baseline activities in order:

- **P** Collect data over a long, representative period
- **Q** Write the operational definition of the metric
- **R** Report the baseline in the problem statement
- **S** Propose the improvement target`,options:["Q → P → R → S","P → Q → R → S","Q → S → P → R","S → Q → P → R"],answer:0,hint:"Define → collect → report → target.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Define what counts, collect long-term data, report the baseline, then set the target."},{title:"Apply",body:"Q → P → R → S."}],final:"Definition → data → baseline → target → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — collecting data before defining the metric gives inconsistent data.","Incorrect — a target cannot be judged before the baseline is known.","Incorrect — setting the target first is the classic sequencing error."],bookRef:"Book p. 213"},{id:"sv-1-1106",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:3,stem:`For an SLA-restoration project, put these signals in the order in which they become available, **earliest first**:

- **P** Monthly SLA compliance
- **Q** P1 tickets still unassigned after 15 minutes
- **R** Quarterly service-credit cost
- **S** Daily count of SLA breaches`,options:["S → Q → P → R","Q → S → P → R","Q → P → S → R","R → P → S → Q"],answer:1,hint:"Leading → lagging: minutes → day → month → quarter.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Leading process signals appear first; lagging outcome and financial results appear last."},{title:"Apply",body:"Unassigned after 15 min (minutes) → daily breaches → monthly compliance → quarterly credits."}],final:"Minutes → day → month → quarter → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — unassigned tickets are visible minutes after logging, before any breach is counted.","","Incorrect — daily breach counts are available before the monthly report.","Incorrect — this is the reverse: most lagging first."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.2"},{id:"sv-1-1107",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these metric activities in DMAIC order:

- **P** Confirm the primary metric improved without compromising the secondary metrics
- **Q** Name the primary and secondary metrics in the charter
- **R** Measure the baseline of the primary metric
- **S** Compare pilot results with the baseline`,options:["R → Q → S → P","Q → S → R → P","Q → R → S → P","Q → R → P → S"],answer:2,hint:"Define → Measure → Improve → Control.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Define names the metrics; Measure sets the baseline; Improve pilots; Control confirms success."},{title:"Apply",body:"Q → R → S → P."}],final:"Charter → baseline → pilot → confirm → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the metrics are named in the charter (Define) before the baseline is measured.","Incorrect — pilot results can only be compared once a baseline exists.","","Incorrect — the final success check comes after the pilot, in Control."],bookRef:"Book pp. 229, 574"},{id:"sv-1-1108",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is a **hard saving**?",options:["Capacity freed, such as engineer hours, with no cost removed","Preventing a future cost that has not yet been paid","Extra income from kept customers or new sales","A measurable fall in actual spending against a baseline, visible in the profit and loss"],answer:3,hint:"Four benefit groups: hard, soft, cost avoidance, revenue.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Hard savings** reduce actual spending against a baseline and show in the P&L (fewer SLA credits, lower contractor invoices)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Freed capacity = soft; future cost prevented = cost avoidance; extra income = revenue growth."}],final:"Hard saving = real spend falls → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — freed capacity with no spend removed is a soft saving.","Incorrect — this is cost avoidance; today’s spend does not fall.","Incorrect — this is revenue growth.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-1109",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which describes **cost avoidance**?",options:["Preventing a future cost that has not yet been incurred","Reducing a cost that is currently being paid","Freeing staff time that is used for other work","Bringing in income sooner through faster billing"],answer:0,hint:"Has the money ever been spent?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Cost avoidance** prevents a future cost — a fine, a planned purchase, a penalty clause from next year."},{title:"Apply",body:"It is valuable, but today’s spend does not fall, so it is not a hard saving."}],final:"Future cost prevented = cost avoidance → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — reducing a cost being paid now is a hard saving.","Incorrect — freed time with no spend removed is a soft saving.","Incorrect — earlier or extra income is revenue growth."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1110",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which benefit is BEST classified as **revenue growth**?",options:["Lower contractor invoices for field repairs","Extra income from enterprise customers who renewed after service improved","Not having to buy extra network capacity planned for next year","Engineer hours freed by fewer escalations"],answer:1,hint:"Revenue = more money coming in, not less going out.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Revenue growth** is extra income from kept customers, new sales or faster billing."},{title:"Apply",body:"Renewals won by better service add income; the others reduce, avoid or free costs."}],final:"Renewal income = revenue growth → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — lower invoices reduce actual spend, a hard saving.","","Incorrect — avoiding a future purchase is cost avoidance.","Incorrect — freed hours with no cost removed are a soft saving."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1111",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"What does the **Verify Financial Impact** step at the end of Control confirm?",options:["That the charter’s estimated benefit has been calculated","That the solution has been piloted successfully","That the expected financial benefit has actually appeared and been confirmed","That the control plan has been handed to the process owner"],answer:2,hint:"The roadmap ends with money, not with paperwork.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s roadmap ends Control with **Verify Financial Impact** (p. 499): confirm the benefit is real."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Estimate = Define; pilot = Improve; hand-over = ownership."}],final:"Verify Financial Impact = benefit really appeared → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — estimating the benefit happens in Define, from the baseline COPQ.","Incorrect — piloting is an Improve activity; it validates, but does not verify realised money.","","Incorrect — hand-over is part of Control, but it confirms ownership, not the money."],bookRef:"Book p. 499"},{id:"sv-1-1112",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book glossary, what does the **Finance representative** provide on a project?",options:["Day-to-day ownership of the improved process","Statistical analysis to verify the root causes","Removal of organisational barriers for the team","An independent evaluation of the project’s hard and/or soft savings"],answer:3,hint:"Glossary p. 585: independent evaluation.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Finance representative gives an **independent evaluation** of a project’s hard and/or soft savings and supports Champions and project leaders (p. 585)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Process owner = keeps the gain; Belt = analysis; Champion = barriers."}],final:"Finance = independent savings evaluation → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this is the process owner’s role.","Incorrect — root-cause analysis is the Belt’s work in Analyze.","Incorrect — removing barriers is the Champion’s role.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-1113",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"At project closure, who is responsible for **keeping the gain** so that the benefits continue after the team disbands?",options:["The process owner","The Finance representative","The Black Belt","The Champion"],answer:0,hint:"Hand-over: who runs the process every day?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"At hand-over the **process owner** keeps the gain, the Champion signs and Finance records the realised figure."},{title:"Apply",body:"Benefits continue only if the person who runs the process owns the control plan."}],final:"Process owner keeps the gain → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — Finance evaluates and records the realised figure; it does not run the process.","Incorrect — the Belt hands over and moves on to the next project.","Incorrect — the Champion signs off and sponsors; daily ownership sits with the process owner."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1114",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"The book lists a Control-phase roadblock linked to benefits capture. Which is it?",options:["The problem statement contains a disguised solution","Financial benefits are not tracked and integrated into the business","The measurement system has not been validated","Root causes have not been verified with data"],answer:1,hint:"Match each roadblock to its phase.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s Control roadblocks include “**financial benefits are not tracked and integrated into the business**” (p. 577)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Disguised solution = Define; MSA = Measure; root cause = Analyze."}],final:"Untracked benefits = Control roadblock → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this is a Define (charter) weakness.","","Incorrect — this is a Measure-phase issue.","Incorrect — this is an Analyze-phase issue."],bookRef:"Book p. 577"},{id:"sv-1-1115",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"At project closure, who does the book’s checklist say must **all agree** that the project is complete?",options:["The Belt, the customer and the regulator","The process owner, the vendor and the Belt","The Champion, the Belt and Finance","The Master Black Belt and the team members"],answer:2,hint:"Book Control checklist p. 578.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The closing checklist asks whether **the Champion, the Belt and Finance** all agree the project is complete (p. 578)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Finance confirms the money; the Champion confirms ownership; the Belt confirms the work."}],final:"Champion + Belt + Finance agree → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — customers and regulators are stakeholders, not the closure sign-off group.","Incorrect — vendors do not sign off project closure; Finance and the Champion do.","","Incorrect — coaching and team roles do not replace the Champion and Finance sign-off."],bookRef:"Book p. 578"},{id:"sv-1-1116",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"At which point are a project’s financial benefits normally **validated**?",options:["In Define, from the baseline COPQ","During Measure, from the data collection plan","Before chartering, from the project selection matrix","After Improve, using pilot data and a method agreed with Finance"],answer:3,hint:"Estimated → validated → realised.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Benefits are **estimated** in Define, **validated** after Improve (pilot data, Finance-agreed method) and **realised** after Control."},{title:"Apply",body:"Validation needs real evidence of the effect, which first exists after the pilot."}],final:"Validated after Improve → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Define produces the estimate, not the validated figure.","Incorrect — a data collection plan contains no proven result.","Incorrect — selection scores rank projects; they prove no benefit.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1117",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why are a project’s realised benefits normally tracked for about **12 months**?",options:["To confirm that the gain lasts and the money really appears, including ramp-up and seasonal effects","Because Finance can book savings only once a year","Because the control plan expires after 12 months","So that the Belt can report soft savings as hard savings"],answer:0,hint:"Realised = sustained and real.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Realised benefits are tracked month by month for about a year."},{title:"Apply",body:"A full year shows whether the gain holds through ramp-up months, peaks and seasons."}],final:"12 months proves the gain lasts → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — tracking is monthly; the reason is proof that the gain lasts.","Incorrect — a control plan continues; it does not expire.","Incorrect — tracking never changes a benefit’s category."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1118",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about **revenue growth** as a project benefit is CORRECT?",options:["It is a fall in actual spending against a baseline","It adds income, for example from kept customers, new sales or faster billing","It is a future cost that the project prevents","It is capacity freed for other work"],answer:1,hint:"Four groups: hard, soft, cost avoidance, revenue.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Revenue growth = extra income (kept customers, new sales, faster billing)."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Spend falls = hard; future cost prevented = cost avoidance; capacity freed = soft."}],final:"Revenue growth = more income → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this describes a hard saving.","","Incorrect — this describes cost avoidance.","Incorrect — this describes a soft saving."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1119",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about **soft savings** is CORRECT?",options:["They must be removed from the project report","They are the same as revenue growth","They are real benefits, even though actual spending does not fall","They reduce a cost line in the profit and loss"],answer:2,hint:"Real but indirect.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Soft savings — freed hours, higher satisfaction, faster decisions — are real value."},{title:"Apply",body:"No spend falls, so they stay soft and are reported separately."}],final:"Soft = real value, no spend reduction → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — soft savings are reported, but separately from hard savings.","Incorrect — revenue growth is extra income; soft savings are freed capacity or indirect gains.","","Incorrect — a reduced cost line is a hard saving."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1120",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about **attributing** savings to a project is CORRECT?",options:["Every cost reduction after go-live belongs to the project","Attribution matters only for soft savings","Finance attributes savings from the target, not from actual data","Savings caused by outside events, such as a seasonal drop in volume, should not be claimed"],answer:3,hint:"Attribute honestly.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Good benefits capture **attributes honestly**: a fall caused by volume, season or a lost customer is not a project benefit."},{title:"Apply",body:"Only the change caused by the solution is claimed."}],final:"Outside causes are not project savings → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — other causes can lower costs at the same time; only the project’s effect counts.","Incorrect — honest attribution applies to every category, especially hard savings.","Incorrect — realised savings come from actual results, not the target.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1121",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"statements",difficulty:1,stem:`Which statements about **hard savings** are CORRECT?

1. They reduce actual spending against a baseline.
2. Lower contractor invoices are an example.
3. Engineer hours freed with no cost removed are an example.
4. Avoiding a fine expected next year is an example.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:0,hint:"Hard = real spend falls now.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 true (definition). 2 true (invoices fall). 3 false (soft). 4 false (cost avoidance)."},{title:"Match the pair",body:"True: 1 and 2."}],final:"True: 1 and 2 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — statement 3 is false: freed hours with no cost removed are soft savings.","Incorrect — statements 3 and 4 are false: they are soft savings and cost avoidance.","Incorrect — statement 4 is false: a future fine avoided is cost avoidance."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1122",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about the **Finance representative** are CORRECT?

1. They own the improved process day to day.
2. They give an independent evaluation of the project’s savings.
3. They support Champions and project leaders.
4. They choose the project’s primary metric.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:1,hint:"Glossary p. 585: independent evaluation; supports Champions and leaders.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 false (process owner). 2 true. 3 true. 4 false."},{title:"Match the pair",body:"True: 2 and 3."}],final:"True: 2 and 3 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statement 1 is false: the process owner runs the process day to day.","","Incorrect — statement 4 is false: the primary metric comes from the charter problem, agreed by the Belt and Champion.","Incorrect — statements 1 and 4 are both false (process owner; charter metric)."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-1123",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about tracking **realised** benefits are CORRECT?

1. Tracking stops at the Improve tollgate.
2. Realised benefits always equal the charter estimate.
3. Ramp-up months are often below the estimate.
4. Realised benefits are normally tracked for about 12 months.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:2,hint:"Realised = after Control, month by month, ~12 months.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 false. 2 false. 3 true (ramp-up). 4 true (~12 months)."},{title:"Match the pair",body:"True: 3 and 4."}],final:"True: 3 and 4 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statements 1 and 2 are both false: tracking continues after Control, and realised often differs from the estimate.","Incorrect — statement 2 is false: realised benefits often differ from the estimate.","","Incorrect — statement 1 is false: tracking continues after Control, for about 12 months."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1124",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

[?] savings are real but indirect, such as engineer hours freed or faster decisions.`,options:["Avoided","Hard","Realised","Soft"],answer:3,hint:"Category of freed capacity.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Soft savings** are real but indirect: hours freed, satisfaction, faster decisions."},{title:"Apply",body:"No spend falls unless the business acts on them."}],final:"Indirect benefit = soft → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — “avoided” costs are future costs prevented, not freed capacity.","Incorrect — hard savings reduce actual spending in the P&L.","Incorrect — “realised” is a status (confirmed after Control), not a category.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1125",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:2,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

Reporting cost avoidance as a [?] saving is a common benefits-capture trap.`,options:["hard","net","realised","soft"],answer:0,hint:"Which category means today’s spend falls?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Cost avoidance prevents a future cost; today’s spending does not fall."},{title:"Apply",body:"Calling it a **hard** saving overstates the financial result — a sourced exam trap."}],final:"Cost avoidance ≠ hard saving → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — net vs gross is about deducting ongoing costs, not about the category trap.","Incorrect — realised is a status after Control, not a benefit category.","Incorrect — the trap is overstating cost avoidance as money already leaving the P&L, i.e. hard."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1126",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which is NOT an example of **cost avoidance**?",options:["Lower monthly invoices from a repair contractor","Not buying extra network capacity that was planned for next year","Avoiding a regulatory fine expected next year","Avoiding extra software licences that would be needed next year"],answer:0,hint:"Future cost prevented vs current cost reduced.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Cost avoidance prevents **future** costs; hard savings cut **current** spending."},{title:"Apply",body:"Lower contractor invoices reduce money being paid now → hard saving, not cost avoidance."}],final:"Lower current invoices = hard saving → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — avoiding a planned future purchase is cost avoidance.","Incorrect — preventing a future fine is cost avoidance.","Incorrect — avoiding a future licence purchase is cost avoidance."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1127",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"Which is NOT a **soft** saving?",options:["Engineer hours freed by fewer repeat visits","Overtime payments that stop because the overtime budget is cut","Higher customer-satisfaction scores","Faster management decisions from a new dashboard"],answer:1,hint:"Has a cost line actually moved?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Soft savings become hard only when the business acts and spending falls."},{title:"Apply",body:"Cutting the overtime budget so payments stop is exactly that → hard saving, not soft."}],final:"Cut overtime budget = hard → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — freed hours with no cost removed are a soft saving.","","Incorrect — satisfaction is real but indirect value, a soft saving.","Incorrect — faster decisions are an indirect, soft benefit."],trap:"Freed hours are soft; stopped payments are hard.",bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1128",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which is NOT one of the statuses a project benefit moves through?",options:["Estimated","Validated","Budgeted","Realised"],answer:2,hint:"Three statuses across DMAIC.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Benefit status: **estimated** (Define) → **validated** (after Improve) → **realised** (after Control)."},{title:"Apply",body:"“Budgeted” is not a project benefit status."}],final:"Estimated → validated → realised → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — benefits are estimated in Define, from the baseline COPQ.","Incorrect — benefits are validated after Improve with pilot data.","","Incorrect — benefits are realised after Control, once the money has appeared."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1129",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"After invoice processing is fixed, the operator stops paying **late-payment charges** to suppliers that it was paying every month. How should Finance classify this benefit?",options:["Cost avoidance","Hard saving","Revenue growth","Soft saving"],answer:1,hint:"Current spend falls?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Hard savings cut actual spending against a baseline — fewer late-payment charges is a book example."},{title:"Apply",body:"Charges paid every month now stop → hard saving."}],final:"Stopped charges = hard saving → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the charges were being paid; stopping them cuts current spend.","","Incorrect — no income is added; a cost disappears.","Incorrect — the cost line really falls, so it is not indirect."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1130",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A project fixes a rating error, so roaming usage that was never billed is now charged to customers. How is this benefit BEST classified?",options:["Cost avoidance","Hard saving","Revenue growth","Soft saving"],answer:2,hint:"More money in vs less money out.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Revenue growth is extra income — here from usage that was previously lost."},{title:"Apply",body:"Nothing is cut or avoided; billing more usage adds income."}],final:"Recovered billing = revenue growth → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — no future cost is prevented; new income is collected.","Incorrect — spending does not fall; income rises.","","Incorrect — the effect is direct income, not freed capacity."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1131",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A project fixes a reporting gap that would have triggered a **contract penalty clause** starting at next year’s renewal. No penalty has ever been paid. How should Finance classify this benefit?",options:["Cost avoidance","Hard saving","Revenue growth","Soft saving"],answer:0,hint:"Future cost prevented.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Preventing a future cost not yet incurred — such as next year’s penalty clause — is cost avoidance."},{title:"Apply",body:"Today’s spending is unchanged, so it is not a hard saving."}],final:"Future penalty prevented = cost avoidance → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — nothing currently paid falls; the penalty was only a future risk.","Incorrect — no income is added.","Incorrect — this prevents a specific future cost rather than freeing capacity."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1132",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A project cuts escalations, and the customer-satisfaction score rises by six points. No budget, invoice or headcount changes. How is this benefit BEST classified?",options:["Cost avoidance","Hard saving","Revenue growth","Soft saving"],answer:3,hint:"Real but indirect value.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Higher satisfaction is real value but indirect → soft."},{title:"Apply",body:"It could later support revenue (renewals), but until income is shown it stays soft."}],final:"Satisfaction gain = soft → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — no specific future cost is shown to be prevented.","Incorrect — no cost line falls, so nothing is visible in the P&L.","Incorrect — no extra income has been shown; satisfaction alone is not revenue.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1133",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"A Black Belt calculates her project’s savings herself and reports them directly to the steering committee without involving Finance. Was this an appropriate action, and why?",options:["Yes, because the Belt knows the project data best","Yes, because Finance reviews only soft savings","No, because only the process owner may report savings","No, because Finance should give an independent evaluation of the savings"],answer:3,hint:"Who validates benefits?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Finance representative provides an **independent evaluation** of savings (p. 585)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Skipping Finance leaves an unchecked, probably optimistic figure."}],final:"No independent Finance check → not appropriate → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — knowing the data does not remove the need for an independent check.","Incorrect — Finance evaluates hard and/or soft savings.","Incorrect — the issue is independence of the evaluation, not who presents it.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-1134",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"Contractor repair costs fell 30% after go-live. Finance notes that the contractor’s **price per job** was also cut in a contract renegotiation that same month. What is the BEST action before savings are claimed?",options:["Claim the full 30% because it happened after go-live","Claim nothing because two changes happened together","Reclassify the whole saving as cost avoidance","Separate the effect of the price cut from the project’s effect"],answer:3,hint:"Attribute honestly.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Only the change caused by the project is a project benefit."},{title:"Apply",body:"Split the fall into price effect (renegotiation) and volume effect (fewer jobs from the solution), and claim only the latter."}],final:"Split price effect from project effect → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — timing alone does not prove the project caused the whole fall.","Incorrect — the project effect can still be measured, e.g. through the number of jobs.","Incorrect — the cost really fell; the issue is attribution, not category.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1135",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A project freed 300 contractor hours a month. From next month, the contractor contract is reduced by the same 300 hours and the invoice falls. The Black Belt reported this as a **hard** saving. Was this an appropriate action, and why?",options:["Yes, because the business acted on the freed capacity and actual spending falls","Yes, because any freed hours count as hard savings","No, because freed hours are always soft savings","No, because contractor savings are cost avoidance"],answer:0,hint:"Soft becomes hard when spend really falls.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Freed capacity is soft until the business acts on it."},{title:"Apply",body:"Here the contract and invoice are reduced → real spend falls → hard saving, so the report was appropriate."}],final:"Acted on + invoice falls = hard → appropriate → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — freed hours stay soft unless spending actually falls.","Incorrect — soft savings become hard when the business acts and a cost line falls.","Incorrect — the invoice was being paid and now falls; nothing future is merely avoided."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1136",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`The project office prints a closure check:

\`\`\`
Project closure check - PRJ-318

Item                                   Status
Control plan handed to process owner   Done
Champion agrees project complete       Yes
Belt agrees project complete           Yes
Finance verifies financial impact      Pending
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is BEST supported?`,options:["The project cannot close yet, because the financial impact has not been verified by Finance","The project can close, because the Champion and the Belt agree","The project should return to Improve to repeat the pilot","The control plan must be rewritten before Finance can review"],answer:0,hint:"Champion + Belt + Finance; last step = Verify Financial Impact.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Only “Finance verifies financial impact” is pending."},{title:"Apply",body:"Closure needs Champion, Belt and Finance agreement (p. 578), and Control ends with Verify Financial Impact (p. 499)."}],final:"Finance pending → not ready to close → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the book’s checklist needs Finance agreement as well.","Incorrect — nothing shows the solution failed; only financial verification is missing.","Incorrect — the control plan is already handed over; it is not the open item."],bookRef:"Book pp. 499, 578"},{id:"sv-1-1137",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Finance supplies this annual benefit summary for the steering pack:

\`\`\`
Benefit summary (OMR per year)

Line                               Value
Gross hard saving (SLA credits)   324,000
Ongoing cost (dashboard licence)   12,000
Net hard saving                   312,000
Soft saving (engineer hours)       48,000
\`\`\`

Which statement about reporting these figures is CORRECT?`,options:["The engineer-hours line should be added to the net hard saving","The engineer-hours line is reported separately from the hard-saving figures","The 324,000 line is the project’s net hard saving","The ongoing licence cost is a soft saving"],answer:1,hint:"Keep categories apart; net = after ongoing costs.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Hard lines: gross 324,000, net 312,000. Soft line: 48,000 engineer hours (no cost removed)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Soft savings are real but reported side by side, never inside the hard total."}],final:"Soft line reported separately → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — adding soft savings to hard savings overstates the money that really leaves the P&L.","","Incorrect — 324,000 is gross; the net line (after ongoing cost) is printed separately.","Incorrect — it is a cost of the solution, deducted to get the net figure."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1138",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"Cost avoidance reduces today’s baseline spending.",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Future cost vs current cost.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Cost avoidance = preventing a cost not yet incurred."},{title:"Apply",body:"Because that cost was never in today’s spending, the baseline does not fall → False."}],final:"Baseline spend unchanged — False → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — cost avoidance prevents a future cost; today’s spending does not fall.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1139",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"Benefits shown in a project charter at the Define tollgate are estimates, not confirmed savings.",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Estimated → validated → realised.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Charter benefits are **estimated** from the baseline COPQ and the goal."},{title:"Apply",body:"They are validated after Improve and realised after Control → true."}],final:"Define = estimates — True → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the statement is true: Define holds estimates; validation and realisation come later."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.3.3"},{id:"sv-1-1140",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these steps for claiming a project saving in order:

- **P** Finance records the realised figure
- **Q** The Belt proposes how the saving will be calculated
- **R** Finance agrees the method and the benefit category
- **S** Actual monthly results are tracked against the baseline`,options:["R → Q → S → P","Q → S → R → P","Q → R → S → P","S → Q → R → P"],answer:2,hint:"Agree the rules, then keep the score.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Agree the method early with Finance, track actual results, then Finance records the realised figure."},{title:"Apply",body:"Q → R → S → P."}],final:"Propose → agree → track → record → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Finance agrees a method the Belt has first proposed.","Incorrect — the method and category should be agreed before results are tracked.","","Incorrect — tracking without an agreed method invites disputes later."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"}];export{e as items};
